s ${90}^{\\circ}$. Find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/f4/f41802149d3e2a8524a47123e6de1bb6aa68e480cf54aafd756085ad52355ede.png)" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110292 1071 "The measure of the angle $\\angle BAD$ is ${90}^{\\circ}.$ Find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/f4/f41802149d3e2a8524a47123e6de1bb6aa68e480cf54aafd756085ad52355ede.png)" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110292 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $\\angle CAD$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overrightarrow{AC}$.\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(\\angle BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD)$.\n- This means that the measure of $\\angle CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(\\angle BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(\\angle BAC)$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 55^\\circ\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110292 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $\\angle CAD.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overrightarrow{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(\\angle BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $\\angle CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(\\angle BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(\\angle BAC).$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 55^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110381 1071 "In the figure below, determine $m\\angle{POR}$.\n\n![](images/0b/0bb38727216cc66882b7ed55fa59c3c700243ad18eb035a3e19a73eeffa81a2a.png)" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110381 1071 "In the figure below, determine $m\\angle{POR}.$\n\n![](images/0b/0bb38727216cc66882b7ed55fa59c3c700243ad18eb035a3e19a73eeffa81a2a.png)" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110381 1074 "![](images/70/70bb672243a7689bd08c2d03ebe19570fdb148182dd7b5137da5be4b91724f03.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\angle{POQ}$ and $\\angle{QOR}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overrightarrow{OQ}$.\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠POR &= m∠POQ + m∠QOR \\\\\n&= 68^{∘} + 37^{∘} \\\\\n&= 105^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110381 1074 "![](images/70/70bb672243a7689bd08c2d03ebe19570fdb148182dd7b5137da5be4b91724f03.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\angle{POQ}$ and $\\angle{QOR}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overrightarrow{OQ}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠POR & =m∠POQ+m∠QOR \\\\  & ={68}^{∘}+{37}^{∘} \\\\  & ={105}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110382 1071 "In the figure below, $m \\angle BAE = {110}^{\\circ}$. Find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/24/245dbaa430eb99aee193110fcd6df2844cfd7cffd2363c561337ac74d18a1fb5.png)" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110382 1071 "In the figure below, $m \\angle BAE = {110}^{\\circ}.$ Find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/24/245dbaa430eb99aee193110fcd6df2844cfd7cffd2363c561337ac74d18a1fb5.png)" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110382 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $\\angle CAD$.\n\nThe measure of $\\angle CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(\\angle BAE)$ and the two smaller known angles $(\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle DAE)$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 110^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 20^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110382 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $\\angle CAD.$\n\nThe measure of $\\angle CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(\\angle BAE)$ and the two smaller known angles $(\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle DAE).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx = 110^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 20^\\circ = 50^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432454 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432456 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432459 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432463 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432453 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432455 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432457 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432461 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432458 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432460 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432462 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432464 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110861 1050 420.0 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110927 1071 "![](images/b8/b884ae8109d723e9277daa7b076d8a4ff52c07c8295e83d360a07bbb7785dd1d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m∠BAD = 90^{∘}$. What equation could be used to find the value of $x$?" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110927 1071 "![](images/b8/b884ae8109d723e9277daa7b076d8a4ff52c07c8295e83d360a07bbb7785dd1d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m∠BAD={90}^{∘}.$ What equation could be used to find the value of $x?$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110927 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠BAC.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠BAC$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠CAD).$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation to determine $x$ is the following:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}-{40}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110954 1071 "![](images/bd/bd99171c2696038851bf0fdf7297d2e4a60791f6b11083af8cd51baf48930b2d.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $COE^(^)$?" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186110954 1071 "![](images/bd/bd99171c2696038851bf0fdf7297d2e4a60791f6b11083af8cd51baf48930b2d.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{^}{COE}?$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110954 1074 "![](images/67/67952e3c3d8b030327cbae686da23d466d509de9171242f5ccc4e502f10813d7.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\overset{^}{COD}$ and $\\overset{^}{DOE}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OD}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\overset{^}{COE} & =m\\overset{^}{COD}+m\\overset{^}{DOE} \\\\  & ={63}^{∘}+{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186111836 1064 "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle.\n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PST &= m∠PSR_(⏟)_(orange angle) + m∠RST_(⏟)_(blue angle) \\\\\n&= 95^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186111836 1064 "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & ={{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186111899 1064 "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠QST &= m∠QSP_(⏟)_(green angle) + m∠PSR_(⏟)_(orange angle) + m∠RST_(⏟)_(blue angle) \\\\\n&= 100^{∘} + 95^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 240^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." 13194139534679 17592186045417 false] [17592186111899 1064 "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QST & ={{m∠QSP}_{⏟}}_{\\text{green angle}}+{{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432375 1080 "{105}^{∘}" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432375 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432375 1083 #uuid "03d7c93e-855d-5840-b285-e8b2de86a4d0" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432376 1080 "{104}^{∘}" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432376 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432376 1083 #uuid "126909ba-3771-5f25-a383-19d63b8ff86c" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432377 1080 "{58}^{∘}" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432377 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432377 1083 #uuid "14bf17c3-9309-5621-b172-5a68815fd9b2" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432378 1080 "{114}^{∘}" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432378 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432378 1083 #uuid "150dcf2e-cf5d-580c-80cf-4de132de6ddb" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432379 1080 "{65}^{∘}" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432379 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432379 1083 #uuid "1899928f-124e-5f54-b642-61246e73c67e" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432380 1080 "{35}^{∘}" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432380 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432380 1083 #uuid "1a2b250c-0086-5b53-9dbb-d1a339f84847" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186432381 1080 "{37}^{∘}" 13194